# Deploy — local first, production on approval

## Default: local only

- Day-to-day work stays on the machine: `python -m http.server 8080` → `http://localhost:8080/planner/index.html`
- **Do not** commit, push, or run Vercel deploy unless the user explicitly asks to deploy / ship / push to production / update Vercel.

## Production path (when user asks to deploy)

1. Run tests: `node planner/.tests/run-tests.js` (must pass).
2. User validates in the browser locally.
3. Run: `powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File scripts/deploy-to-vercel.ps1`  
   — or commit **only** deployable paths and `git push origin main` if the user asked you to commit deploy changes.

## What goes to Git / Vercel

| Deploy | Keep local only |
|--------|-----------------|
| `planner/**` (app) | `conversations/` |
| `vercel.json` | `*.csv`, generated `sprint-*.html` |
| `README.planner.md` | `force-app/`, SFDC config |
| `scripts/deploy-to-vercel.ps1` | `.cursor/hooks/.user-buffer.txt` |
| `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` | Salesforce `package.json`, husky, etc. |

## Vercel

- Static site; `outputDirectory` = `planner` (see root `vercel.json`).
- Production URL serves `/` = `index.html` (not `/planner/index.html`).
- Branch **main** → production (connect repo once in Vercel dashboard).

## Commits

- Deploy commits: concise message focused on why (e.g. "Ship multi-team tabs and sticky action bar").
- Never commit secrets (`.env`, credentials).
